course: dictionary
lesson: 13089
title: Finances: money and how it is managed
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Finances is a word about money. As a noun, it means the money a person, family, business or country has, and how it is managed, or looked after and spent. «The family finances were tight after Dad lost his job.» Tight means there was not much spare money, so the family had to be careful.
explain: Finances can also be a verb, a doing word, when it ends in s. «The school club finances the new kit with money from its bake sale» means the club pays for the kit.
explain: To work out the meaning, read the clues around the word. Ask: what word would fit here? In «Mum checks the family finances each month», you can swap in "money and spending" and the sentence still makes sense. That tells you your meaning is right.
explain: Look at the phrases too. «His finances are in a mess» means his money matters are muddled. «Sort out your finances» means put your money matters in order.
fact: «Finances» means the money a person, family, business or country has and how it is managed.
fact: As a verb, «finances» means pays for something, as in «The bank finances the new bridge».
fact: A family's finances are how much money it has and how it spends it.
fact: Phrases to know: family finances, sort out your finances, his finances are in a mess.
fact: A good check is to swap in "money and spending" or "pays for" and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «The family finances were tight after Dad lost his job.» What does finances mean here? | The dinner the family eats together | The name of the family's car | *The money a family has and how it is managed | A map of the family's home
exercise: «The school club finances the new kit with money from its bake sale.» What does finances mean in this sentence? | Loses track of something | Sells the kit to other schools | *Pays for something | Makes a dull noise
exercise: «Sort out your finances» means | Climb up a wall | Paint the kitchen a new colour | *Put your money matters in order | Count the stars in the sky
test: «His finances are in a mess, so he cannot pay the rent this month.» What does a mess suggest about his finances? | His money is tidy and spare | *His money is muddled and out of order | He has a lot of mess to clean up | He gave all his money away
test: Mum says, «The bank finances the new bridge.» What does finances mean here? | Puts the bridge on a map | *Pays for something | Lends a book to the library | Counts coins in a jar
exam: Which meaning matches finances as a noun? | *The money a person or family has and how it is managed | Pays for a bridge | A type of bird | A loud noise
